import{n as e,t}from"./expand-B9sP1tsw.js";var n=[[3,1,`Which word is a noun in this sentence? "The dog runs fast."`,`dog`,[`runs`,`fast`,`the`],`A noun names a person, place, animal or thing. 'Dog' is the animal.`],[3,1,`Which word means the opposite of 'hot'?`,`cold`,[`warm`,`sunny`,`wet`],`Opposites are called antonyms. Hot and cold are antonyms.`],[3,1,`Which word is a verb in this sentence? "The bird sings."`,`sings`,[`bird`,`the`,`song`],`A verb is an action word. 'Sings' is what the bird does.`],[3,1,`What is the plural of 'cat'?`,`cats`,[`cat's`,`cates`,`catts`],`For most words, add -s to make the plural.`],[3,1,`How many vowels are in the word 'apple'?`,`Two`,[`One`,`Three`,`Four`],`The vowels in 'apple' are a and e.`],[3,2,`What is the plural of 'child'?`,`children`,[`childs`,`childes`,`childrens`],`'Child' is an irregular word, so its plural is 'children'.`],[3,2,`Which word rhymes with 'light'?`,`night`,[`lamp`,`late`,`line`],`Rhyming words have the same ending sound: light, night, bright.`],[3,2,`Which sentence starts and ends correctly?`,`We went to the park.`,[`we went to the park`,`We went to the park`,`we Went to the park.`],`A sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark.`],[3,2,`Which word is a synonym (similar meaning) for 'big'?`,`large`,[`tiny`,`thin`,`short`],`Synonyms have almost the same meaning. Big and large are synonyms.`],[3,2,`Which word is an adjective in: "The red balloon floated away."`,`red`,[`balloon`,`floated`,`away`],`An adjective describes a noun. 'Red' tells us about the balloon.`],[3,3,`Choose the correct word: "I ___ my homework yesterday."`,`did`,[`done`,`do`,`doing`],`'Yesterday' tells us it happened in the past, so we use 'did'.`],[3,3,`Which word is spelled correctly?`,`friend`,[`freind`,`frend`,`frind`],`Remember: 'i' before 'e' in friend — a friend is there till the end.`],[3,3,`Which punctuation mark ends a question?`,`A question mark (?)`,[`A full stop (.)`,`A comma (,)`,`An exclamation mark (!)`],`Questions end with a question mark.`],[3,3,`Choose the correct word: "There are two ___ in the garden."`,`birds`,[`bird`,`birdes`,`bird's`],`Two or more means we need the plural: birds.`],[3,3,`Which word means the same as 'happy'?`,`joyful`,[`gloomy`,`angry`,`sleepy`],`Joyful and happy are synonyms.`],[4,1,`Which of these is a proper noun?`,`London`,[`city`,`river`,`mountain`],`Proper nouns name specific people or places and start with a capital letter.`],[4,1,`Which word is an adverb in: "She ran quickly."`,`quickly`,[`She`,`ran`,`She ran`],`An adverb tells us how an action is done. 'Quickly' describes how she ran.`],[4,1,`What do we call a group of words that makes a complete thought?`,`A sentence`,[`A syllable`,`A prefix`,`A title`],`A sentence has a subject and a verb and makes sense on its own.`],[4,1,`How many syllables are in the word 'banana'?`,`Three`,[`Two`,`Four`,`One`],`Ba-na-na has three beats, or syllables.`],[4,2,`Which punctuation mark shows ownership, as in 'the dog's bone'?`,`An apostrophe`,[`A comma`,`A colon`,`A hyphen`],`An apostrophe followed by s shows that something belongs to someone.`],[4,2,`Choose the correct word: "They ___ going to the zoo."`,`are`,[`is`,`am`,`be`],`'They' is plural, so we use 'are'.`],[4,2,`What is the past tense of 'run'?`,`ran`,[`runned`,`running`,`runs`],`'Run' is irregular. Its past tense is 'ran'.`],[4,2,`What is the opposite of 'ancient'?`,`modern`,[`old`,`historic`,`antique`],`Ancient means very old; modern means new or of today.`],[4,2,`Which word is spelled correctly?`,`because`,[`becuase`,`becouse`,`becawse`],`Big Elephants Can Always Understand Small Elephants: B-E-C-A-U-S-E.`],[4,2,`What does the prefix 'un-' mean in 'unhappy'?`,`Not`,[`Again`,`Before`,`Very`],`The prefix un- means 'not'. Unhappy means not happy.`],[4,3,`Which sentence uses a comma correctly?`,`After lunch, we played outside.`,[`After, lunch we played outside.`,`After lunch we, played outside.`,`After lunch we played, outside.`],`A comma goes after an opening phrase like 'After lunch'.`],[4,3,`Which word sounds the same as 'their' but is spelled differently?`,`there`,[`thair`,`these`,`them`],`Words that sound the same but are spelled differently are homophones: their, there, they're.`],[4,3,`Which word has a silent letter?`,`knife`,[`frog`,`plant`,`lamp`],`The k in 'knife' is silent.`],[4,3,`Which of these is a linking verb?`,`is`,[`jump`,`sing`,`build`],`Linking verbs such as 'is' connect the subject to a description rather than showing action.`],[4,3,`Which sentence is a command?`,`Close the door.`,[`Did you close the door?`,`The door is closed.`,`What a loud door!`],`A command tells someone to do something.`],[5,1,`What do we call a word that describes a noun?`,`An adjective`,[`A verb`,`An adverb`,`A pronoun`],`Adjectives describe nouns: a tall tree, a sweet apple.`],[5,1,`Which word is a synonym for 'begin'?`,`start`,[`finish`,`stop`,`pause`],`Begin and start mean the same thing.`],[5,1,`Which punctuation mark goes at the end of an exclamation?`,`!`,[`?`,`,`,`;`],`An exclamation mark shows strong feeling or surprise.`],[5,2,`What is a simile?`,`A comparison using 'like' or 'as'`,[`A comparison saying one thing IS another`,`A word that sounds like its meaning`,`A very long sentence`],`Example of a simile: 'as brave as a lion'.`],[5,2,`What is a metaphor?`,`A comparison that says one thing IS another`,[`A comparison using like or as`,`Repeating a starting sound`,`A question with no answer`],`'Time is a thief' is a metaphor.`],[5,2,`Which sentence contains a simile?`,`He ran like the wind.`,[`He is a lion in battle.`,`The wind whispered.`,`Time is money.`],`'Like the wind' compares his running to the wind using 'like'.`],[5,2,`Which word is a conjunction in: "I wanted to play, but it rained."`,`but`,[`wanted`,`play`,`rained`],`Conjunctions join ideas together: and, but, or, because.`],[5,2,`What does the prefix 're-' mean in 'rewrite'?`,`Again`,[`Not`,`Before`,`Under`],`Re- means 'again'. Rewrite means write again.`],[5,2,`Choose the correct word: "The dog wagged ___ tail."`,`its`,[`it's`,`its'`,`it is`],`'Its' shows possession. 'It's' always means 'it is' or 'it has'.`],[5,3,`Which sentence is in the past tense?`,`She walked to school.`,[`She walks to school.`,`She will walk to school.`,`She is walking to school.`],`Walked is the past tense of walk.`],[5,3,`What is personification?`,`Giving human qualities to non-human things`,[`Exaggerating for effect`,`Repeating a starting sound`,`Comparing with like or as`],`'The wind whispered' gives the wind a human action.`],[5,3,`Which word is an antonym of 'generous'?`,`stingy`,[`kind`,`giving`,`helpful`],`Generous people share freely; stingy people don't.`],[5,3,`What is an idiom?`,`A phrase whose meaning isn't the same as its literal words`,[`A short poem with three lines and a set number of syllables`,`A word that sounds like the noise it describes`,`A sentence that gives an order or makes a request`],`'It's raining cats and dogs' means it's raining hard.`],[5,3,`What is the suffix in the word 'careful'?`,`-ful`,[`care`,`-ar`,`-ef`],`A suffix is added to the end of a word. -ful means 'full of'.`],[5,2,`What is the main idea of a paragraph?`,`What the paragraph is mostly about`,[`The longest sentence`,`The first word`,`The title of the book`],`The main idea is the big point that all the other sentences support.`],[6,1,`When an author writes to explain something, their purpose is to…`,`inform`,[`persuade`,`entertain`,`confuse`],`Authors write to inform, persuade or entertain.`],[6,1,`What is the root word of 'unhappiness'?`,`happy`,[`un`,`ness`,`happ`],`Take away the prefix un- and the suffix -ness and you are left with 'happy'.`],[6,1,`Which part of a book lists its chapters in order?`,`The table of contents`,[`The glossary`,`The index`,`The dedication`],`The table of contents shows chapter titles and page numbers.`],[6,2,`What is alliteration?`,`Repeating the same starting sound in nearby words`,[`Words that sound like noises`,`Comparing using like`,`Giving things human traits`],`'Peter Piper picked a peck' is alliteration.`],[6,2,`Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?`,`I like tea; she likes coffee.`,[`I like; tea she likes coffee.`,`I like tea she; likes coffee.`,`I; like tea she likes coffee.`],`A semicolon joins two closely related complete sentences.`],[6,2,`Words with the same spelling but different meanings (like 'bat') are…`,`Homonyms`,[`Synonyms`,`Antonyms`,`Acronyms`],`A bat can be an animal or something you hit a ball with.`],[6,2,`In which person is: "I walked to the shop"?`,`First person`,[`Second person`,`Third person`,`Fourth person`],`'I' means first person.`],[6,2,`Which sentence has correct subject-verb agreement?`,`She and I are friends.`,[`She and I is friends.`,`She and I am friends.`,`She and I be friends.`],`A compound subject with 'and' is plural, so it takes 'are'.`],[6,3,`What is the theme of a story?`,`The big message or lesson`,[`The setting`,`The main character's name`,`The first sentence`],`A theme is an idea the story explores, like friendship or courage.`],[6,3,`Which of these is an example of onomatopoeia?`,`Buzz`,[`Quickly`,`Beautiful`,`Between`],`Onomatopoeia words sound like what they mean: buzz, splash, bang.`],[6,3,`Which pronoun replaces 'Maria and Sam' as the subject of a sentence?`,`They`,[`Them`,`Their`,`Theirs`],`'They' is the subject pronoun for more than one person.`],[6,3,`A story told with 'I' from inside a character's mind uses…`,`First-person narration`,[`Third-person narration`,`Second-person narration`,`A summary`],`First-person narrators say 'I' and share their own thoughts.`],[6,3,`Which word best completes: "The scientist ___ the results carefully before sharing them."`,`examined`,[`examine`,`examining`,`examines`],`'Before sharing' shows the action finished in the past, so 'examined' fits.`],[6,3,`What is the plural of 'analysis'?`,`analyses`,[`analysises`,`analysi`,`analysis's`],`Words ending in -is often change to -es in the plural: analysis → analyses.`],[7,1,`What is the setting of a story?`,`When and where it takes place`,[`Who the characters are`,`How the story ends`,`What the title means`],`Setting is the time and place of a story.`],[7,1,`Which word is spelled correctly?`,`necessary`,[`neccessary`,`necesary`,`necessery`],`Necessary has one collar (c) and two sleeves (s).`],[7,2,`What does 'ambiguous' mean?`,`Having more than one possible meaning`,[`Having one clear and exact meaning`,`Being very large and hard to measure`,`Being very old and no longer used`],`An ambiguous sentence can be understood in different ways.`],[7,2,`Which sentence is an example of hyperbole?`,`I've told you a million times!`,[`She sings like an angel.`,`The stars danced.`,`Buzz went the bee.`],`Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration.`],[7,2,`Which word is a preposition in: "The cat sat on the mat."`,`on`,[`cat`,`sat`,`mat`],`Prepositions show position or relationship: on, in, under, beside.`],[7,2,`What does the word 'benevolent' mean?`,`Kind and generous`,[`Angry`,`Careless`,`Sleepy`],`'Bene' means good, so benevolent describes someone who wishes others well.`],[7,2,`What is the plural of 'criterion'?`,`criteria`,[`criterions`,`criterias`,`criterium`],`'Criterion' comes from Greek, so its plural is 'criteria'.`],[7,2,`What is a dependent (subordinate) clause?`,`A group of words with a subject and verb that can't stand alone`,[`A group of words with no verb that names a person or thing`,`A complete sentence that can stand on its own as a thought`,`A list of describing words that come before a noun`],`'Although it rained' has a subject and verb but is not a complete thought.`],[7,3,`What is irony?`,`When the outcome is the opposite of what is expected`,[`When two things are compared using 'like' or 'as'`,`When the same first sound is repeated in nearby words`,`When an object is described as if it were a person`],`A fire station burning down is ironic.`],[7,3,`Which sentence is written in the passive voice?`,`The window was broken by the ball.`,[`The ball broke the window.`,`The boy broke the window.`,`Someone is breaking the window.`],`In the passive voice, the thing receiving the action is the subject.`],[7,3,`Which is a run-on sentence?`,`I love reading I read every day.`,[`I love reading, and I read every day.`,`I love reading. I read every day.`,`I love reading; I read every day.`],`A run-on joins two complete sentences without punctuation or a joining word.`],[7,3,`What does the Latin root 'bene' mean, as in 'benefit'?`,`Good`,[`Bad`,`Small`,`Under`],`Bene means 'good' or 'well'.`],[7,3,`What is a connotation?`,`The feeling a word suggests beyond its literal meaning`,[`A word's spelling`,`A word's origin`,`A word's number of syllables`],`'Home' and 'house' both mean a building, but 'home' feels warmer.`],[7,3,`What is a thesis statement?`,`A sentence stating the main argument of an essay`,[`The last line of a story`,`A list of sources`,`A quotation`],`The thesis tells the reader what the essay will prove or explain.`],[8,1,`What is a protagonist?`,`The main character of a story`,[`The villain`,`The setting`,`The narrator's friend`],`The protagonist is the central character whose journey drives the plot.`],[8,1,`Which word is spelled correctly?`,`separate`,[`seperate`,`separete`,`seprate`],`There's 'a rat' in sep-a-rate.`],[8,1,`A five-line humorous poem with an AABBA rhyme scheme is a…`,`limerick`,[`sonnet`,`haiku`,`elegy`],`Limericks are short, funny poems with a bouncy rhythm.`],[8,2,`What is the difference between 'affect' and 'effect'?`,`Affect is usually a verb; effect is usually a noun`,[`They mean exactly the same`,`Affect is a noun; effect is a verb`,`Both are only nouns`],`Weather can affect your mood (verb). The effect of the weather is a good mood (noun).`],[8,2,`What is foreshadowing?`,`Hints about what will happen later in a story`,[`A summary at the end`,`A character's backstory`,`A change of narrator`],`Dark clouds before a sad event are foreshadowing.`],[8,2,`Which sentence uses 'whom' correctly?`,`To whom did you give the book?`,[`Whom is going to the store?`,`Whom called you yesterday?`,`Whom are you?`],`Use 'whom' as an object, after a preposition like 'to'.`],[8,2,`Which word is a synonym of 'meticulous'?`,`careful`,[`careless`,`hasty`,`lazy`],`A meticulous person pays close attention to detail.`],[8,2,`What does 'inference' mean in reading?`,`A conclusion drawn from clues and evidence`,[`A word's definition`,`A summary of the plot`,`A spelling rule`],`Readers infer by combining what the text says with what they already know.`],[8,3,`Which of these is an oxymoron?`,`A deafening silence`,[`A loud noise`,`A quiet whisper`,`A big elephant`],`An oxymoron joins two opposite ideas, like deafening + silence.`],[8,3,`What is a poem of 14 lines called?`,`A sonnet`,[`A haiku`,`A limerick`,`A ballad`],`Sonnets have 14 lines in a set rhyme scheme.`],[8,3,`What is an allusion?`,`A brief reference to a famous person, place or event`,[`A long speech given by one character alone on stage`,`A phrase repeated at the start of several lines`,`A deliberate exaggeration used to make a point`],`Calling a clever plan 'a Trojan horse' is an allusion.`],[8,3,`What is the climax of a story?`,`The turning point where tension is highest`,[`The very first scene`,`The final sentence`,`The author's biography`],`The climax is the moment of greatest tension, before things start to resolve.`],[8,3,`Which of these is a rhetorical question?`,`Who wouldn't want to be happy?`,[`What time is it?`,`Where is the library?`,`How old are you?`],`A rhetorical question is asked for effect, not because an answer is expected.`],[8,3,`Which sentence has a dangling modifier?`,`Walking to school, the rain started.`,[`Walking to school, I got wet in the rain.`,`As I walked to school, the rain started.`,`The rain started while I walked to school.`],`'Walking to school' seems to describe 'the rain', which doesn't make sense.`],[8,3,`Which sentence is a complex sentence?`,`Although it was late, we kept studying.`,[`We studied.`,`We studied and we ate.`,`We studied; we ate.`],`A complex sentence has a dependent clause ('Although it was late') plus an independent one.`]],r=[[[3,1,`grammar`,`Which word in 'We visited the zoo on Sunday' names a place?`,`zoo`,[`visited`,`Sunday`,`We`],`Zoo is a place. Sunday is a day (a time), and visited is the action.`],[3,1,`grammar`,`Find the action word in 'The frog jumped into the pond.'`,`jumped`,[`frog`,`into`,`pond`],`Jumped tells us what the frog did, so it is the verb (action word).`],[3,1,`grammar`,`Which of these words is a pronoun?`,`she`,[`table`,`quickly`,`green`],`A pronoun such as she, he, it or they stands in for a noun.`],[3,2,`grammar`,`Yesterday I ___ an apple. Which word fits the gap?`,`ate`,[`eated`,`eat`,`eaten`],`Eat is irregular: today I eat, yesterday I ate, and I have eaten.`],[3,2,`grammar`,`Which of these is a full sentence, not just a phrase?`,`The cat is asleep.`,[`the fluffy cat`,`under the warm blanket`,`very sleepy today`],`A sentence has a subject (the cat) and a verb (is) and makes a complete thought.`],[3,2,`grammar`,`Which of these words should always start with a capital letter?`,`monday`,[`apple`,`river`,`pencil`],`Days of the week are names, so they take a capital letter: Monday.`],[3,3,`grammar`,`Which of these words is an adjective?`,`shiny`,[`run`,`quickly`,`and`],`Shiny describes a noun, as in 'a shiny coin'.`],[3,3,`grammar`,`How do you make 'box' plural?`,`boxes`,[`boxs`,`boxies`,`boxen`],`Words ending in x, s, sh or ch add -es: boxes, buses, dishes, churches.`],[4,1,`grammar`,`Which of these is a collective noun?`,`flock`,[`bird`,`fly`,`feather`],`A collective noun names a group: a flock of birds, a herd of cows, a team of players.`],[4,2,`grammar`,`Which verb completes 'My brother and I ___ playing football'?`,`are`,[`is`,`am`,`be`],`'My brother and I' is two people (plural), so the verb is are.`],[4,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence is in the future tense?`,`We will visit Grandpa tomorrow.`,[`We visited Grandpa yesterday.`,`We are visiting Grandpa now.`,`We visit Grandpa every week.`],`Will + verb, together with 'tomorrow', shows the future.`],[4,2,`grammar`,`What is the comparative form of 'tall'?`,`taller`,[`tallest`,`more tall`,`tallier`],`Comparatives compare two things (taller); superlatives compare three or more (tallest).`],[4,3,`grammar`,`Which word is the superlative of 'good'?`,`best`,[`better`,`goodest`,`most good`],`Good is irregular: good, better, best.`],[4,3,`grammar`,`Which of these is a possessive pronoun?`,`mine`,[`me`,`I`,`myself`],`Mine shows ownership on its own: 'The red bag is mine.'`],[4,2,`grammar`,`An adverb can describe how a verb is done.`,`True`,[`False`],`In 'She sang softly', the adverb softly tells us how she sang.`],[5,1,`grammar`,`Which of these words is a determiner?`,`those`,[`run`,`green`,`slowly`],`Determiners such as the, a, this, those and some come before a noun to point it out.`],[5,1,`grammar`,`Which sentence is a question?`,`Have you finished your work?`,[`Finish your work now.`,`I have finished my work.`,`What a lot of work!`],`A question asks something and ends with a question mark.`],[5,2,`grammar`,`What is the subject of 'The tall girl kicked the ball'?`,`The tall girl`,[`kicked`,`the ball`,`tall`],`The subject is who or what does the action: the tall girl did the kicking.`],[5,2,`grammar`,`Which of these words is a modal verb?`,`might`,[`walked`,`table`,`under`],`Modal verbs such as can, might, must and should show how likely or necessary something is.`],[5,3,`grammar`,`In 'She sings beautifully', what does 'beautifully' tell us?`,`How she sings`,[`When she sings`,`Where she sings`,`Who is singing`],`Beautifully is an adverb of manner: it describes the way she sings.`],[6,1,`grammar`,`Which sentence uses the present perfect tense?`,`I have finished my homework.`,[`I finished my homework.`,`I am finishing my homework.`,`I will finish my homework.`],`The present perfect uses have/has + past participle: have finished.`],[6,1,`grammar`,`Which of these is an abstract noun?`,`courage`,[`teacher`,`river`,`bicycle`],`Abstract nouns name ideas or feelings you cannot touch: courage, joy, freedom.`],[6,2,`grammar`,`Which of these is a compound sentence?`,`I wanted to swim, but the pool was closed.`,[`Because the pool was closed.`,`The pool was closed on Monday.`,`After the swim, we ate lunch.`],`A compound sentence joins two main clauses with a conjunction such as and, but or so.`],[6,2,`grammar`,`Fewer or less? Which sentence gets it right?`,`There are fewer apples in this box.`,[`There is fewer water in the bottle.`,`I have fewer time than you.`,`She drank fewer milk today.`],`Use fewer with things you can count (apples) and less with things you cannot (water, time, milk).`],[6,3,`grammar`,`A verb can be more than one word, as in 'is running'.`,`True`,[`False`],`Helping verbs combine with main verbs: is running, has eaten, will be going.`],[7,1,`grammar`,`Which sentence is in the active voice?`,`The chef cooked the meal.`,[`The meal was cooked by the chef.`,`The meal had been cooked.`,`The meal is being cooked.`],`In the active voice the subject does the action: the chef cooked.`],[7,2,`grammar`,`In 'The boy who won smiled', which word is the relative pronoun?`,`who`,[`boy`,`won`,`smiled`],`Relative pronouns (who, which, that, whose) start a clause that tells us more about a noun.`],[7,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence uses the subjunctive mood?`,`If I were taller, I would play basketball.`,[`If I was taller, I play basketball.`,`I am taller than my brother.`,`When I grow taller, I will play.`],`The subjunctive 'were' is used for imagined or wished-for situations.`],[7,3,`grammar`,`What is a gerund?`,`A verb form ending in -ing used as a noun`,[`A word that joins two clauses`,`A noun that names a group`,`An adjective that compares two things`],`In 'Swimming is fun', swimming is a gerund: it is the subject of the sentence.`],[7,3,`grammar`,`Identify the object in 'Leila painted a beautiful picture.'`,`a beautiful picture`,[`Leila`,`painted`,`Leila painted`],`The object receives the action: what did Leila paint? A beautiful picture.`],[8,1,`grammar`,`What does a word such as 'however' do when it joins two sentences?`,`It links ideas and shows how they relate`,[`It names a person or place`,`It describes a noun`,`It replaces the verb`],`Conjunctive adverbs (however, therefore, meanwhile) show contrast, result or time between ideas.`],[8,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence uses 'who's' correctly?`,`Who's coming to the party?`,[`Who's book is this?`,`Whose coming to the party?`,`Who's car is outside?`],`Who's means 'who is'. Whose shows ownership: whose book, whose car.`],[8,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence has parallel structure?`,`She likes reading, writing and drawing.`,[`She likes reading, to write and drawing.`,`She likes to read, writing and to draw.`,`She likes reading, writes and to draw.`],`Items in a list should share the same grammatical form: reading, writing, drawing.`],[8,3,`grammar`,`What is an appositive?`,`A noun phrase placed next to a noun to rename it`,[`A verb that shows the future`,`A word that joins two clauses`,`A sentence that gives an order`],`In 'My sister, a doctor, lives in Khulna', a doctor is an appositive.`],[8,1,`grammar`,`Every clause contains a verb.`,`True`,[`False`],`A clause is a group of words with a subject and a verb; a phrase may have no verb.`],[3,1,`punctuation`,`Which sentence needs a question mark at the end?`,`Where is my hat`,[`I like my hat`,`My hat is red`,`Put on your hat`],`It asks something, so it should end with a question mark: Where is my hat?`],[3,2,`punctuation`,`Where do we always use a capital letter?`,`At the start of every sentence`,[`After every comma`,`At the end of a sentence`,`In the middle of each word`],`Sentences begin with a capital letter, and so do names and the word I.`],[3,1,`punctuation`,`Every sentence should end with a punctuation mark.`,`True`,[`False`],`Sentences end with a full stop, a question mark or an exclamation mark.`],[4,1,`punctuation`,`What is the job of commas in a list?`,`To separate the items`,[`To end the sentence`,`To show a question`,`To show who owns something`],`Commas separate items in a list: 'eggs, milk, bread and rice'.`],[4,2,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses speech marks correctly?`,`"Come here," said Mum.`,[`"Come here, said Mum."`,`Come here," said Mum.`,`"Come here, said" Mum.`],`Only the words actually spoken go inside the speech marks, with the comma inside too.`],[4,2,`punctuation`,`How is the short form of 'do not' written?`,`don't`,[`dont`,`do'nt`,`don,t`],`The apostrophe goes where the missing letter (o) was: don't.`],[5,1,`punctuation`,`Why is there an apostrophe in 'can't'?`,`It shows that letters have been left out`,[`It shows that something is owned`,`It shows that there is a list`,`It shows that it is a question`],`Can't is short for cannot; the apostrophe marks the missing letters.`],[5,2,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses brackets correctly?`,`My uncle (who lives in Sylhet) is visiting.`,[`My uncle who (lives in Sylhet) is visiting.`,`(My uncle) who lives in Sylhet is visiting.`,`My uncle who lives (in) Sylhet is visiting.`],`Brackets hold extra information that the sentence still makes sense without.`],[5,3,`punctuation`,`Which sentence shows that several dogs own the toys?`,`The dogs' toys were everywhere.`,[`The dog's toys were everywhere.`,`The dogs toy's were everywhere.`,`The dogs's toys were everywhere.`],`For a plural noun ending in s, the apostrophe goes after the s: the dogs' toys.`],[6,1,`punctuation`,`When is a colon often used?`,`Before a list or an explanation`,[`At the end of every question`,`Instead of a capital letter`,`Between every two words`],`'You need three things: a pen, a ruler and a rubber.'`],[6,2,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses a hyphen correctly?`,`She is a well-known author.`,[`She is a well known-author.`,`She-is a well known author.`,`She is a-well known author.`],`Well-known is a compound adjective before a noun, so its two words are joined with a hyphen.`],[7,1,`punctuation`,`What does an ellipsis (…) usually show?`,`Words left out, or a pause`,[`A strong command`,`A list of three items`,`The end of a question`],`'I was going to say… never mind.' The dots show a trailing-off or missing words.`],[7,2,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses a dash for emphasis?`,`There was only one thing left to do – run!`,[`There was – only one thing left to do run!`,`There was only one – thing left to do run!`,`There – was only one thing left to do run!`],`A dash can set apart the important, surprising part at the end of a sentence.`],[8,2,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses commas correctly around extra information?`,`My best friend, who lives next door, plays the drums.`,[`My best friend who lives, next door plays the drums.`,`My best friend, who lives next door plays, the drums.`,`My, best friend who lives next door, plays the drums.`],`A pair of commas encloses the extra clause, like brackets would.`],[8,3,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses an apostrophe correctly?`,`The children's books are on the shelf.`,[`The childrens' books are on the shelf.`,`The childrens books' are on the shelf.`,`The children's book's are on the shelf.`],`Children is already plural without an s, so its possessive is children's.`]],[[3,1,`vocabulary`,`The giant had enormous feet. What does enormous mean?`,`Very big`,[`Very small`,`Very fast`,`Very cold`],`Enormous means extremely large, like a giant's feet.`],[3,1,`vocabulary`,`The glass was full. Which word is the opposite of full?`,`empty`,[`heavy`,`clean`,`round`],`A full glass has no room left; an empty glass has nothing in it.`],[3,2,`vocabulary`,`Pick the word that rhymes with lake and make.`,`bake`,[`back`,`like`,`leak`],`Lake, make and bake all end with the same sound: -ake.`],[3,2,`vocabulary`,`If you feel tired, you feel…`,`sleepy and in need of rest`,[`very hungry`,`full of energy`,`cold and wet`],`Tired means you need to rest or sleep.`],[3,1,`vocabulary`,`A library is a place where you can borrow books.`,`True`,[`False`],`Libraries lend books for free; a bookshop sells them.`],[4,1,`vocabulary`,`The thief crept silently past the guard. What does silently mean?`,`Without making a sound`,[`Very loudly`,`Very quickly`,`Carrying a torch`],`Silent means with no sound; silently is how the thief moved.`],[4,2,`vocabulary`,`Preheat, preview and prepay all begin with pre-. What does pre- mean?`,`Before`,[`After`,`Again`,`Not`],`To preheat an oven is to heat it before cooking.`],[4,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word is a homophone of 'flour'?`,`flower`,[`floor`,`flare`,`flaw`],`Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings: you bake with flour and smell a flower.`],[4,3,`vocabulary`,`What is a synonym?`,`A word with the same or a similar meaning`,[`A word with the opposite meaning`,`A word that names a place`,`A word that rhymes with another`],`Quick and fast are synonyms. Opposites are antonyms.`],[4,3,`vocabulary`,`Choose the antonym of 'brave'.`,`cowardly`,[`bold`,`fearless`,`daring`],`Bold, fearless and daring are synonyms of brave; cowardly means the opposite.`],[4,2,`vocabulary`,`Polite means the same as rude.`,`False`,[`True`],`They are opposites: polite people use good manners; rude people do not.`],[5,1,`vocabulary`,`The puppy was curious about the box. What does curious mean here?`,`Eager to find out`,[`Afraid of it`,`Bored by it`,`Angry at it`],`A curious puppy wants to sniff and explore the box.`],[5,2,`vocabulary`,`Which suffix turns the word kind into a noun?`,`-ness`,[`-ly`,`-er`,`-ful`],`Kind + -ness = kindness, a noun. Kindly is an adverb and kinder compares.`],[5,2,`vocabulary`,`What does transparent mean in 'a transparent plastic bag'?`,`See-through`,[`Very strong`,`Brightly coloured`,`Waterproof`],`You can see through a transparent bag, like clear glass.`],[5,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to make something bigger?`,`enlarge`,[`reduce`,`shrink`,`divide`],`The prefix en- with large gives enlarge: to make larger.`],[5,3,`vocabulary`,`Which word could replace said to show that someone was angry?`,`snapped`,[`whispered`,`giggled`,`sighed`],`'Stop it!' she snapped. Snapped suggests a sharp, angry tone.`],[5,2,`vocabulary`,`The bi- in bicycle means two.`,`True`,[`False`],`A bicycle has two wheels; bilingual people speak two languages.`],[6,1,`vocabulary`,`The old bridge was fragile. What does fragile mean?`,`Easily broken`,[`Very heavy`,`Newly built`,`Very long`],`Fragile things, like glass or an old bridge, can break easily.`],[6,2,`vocabulary`,`He was reluctant to leave the party. What does reluctant mean?`,`Unwilling`,[`Eager`,`Unable`,`Delighted`],`A reluctant person does something without wanting to.`],[6,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word combines the Greek parts tele (far) and scope (look)?`,`telescope`,[`television`,`telephone`,`microscope`],`A telescope lets you look at things far away. Micro- means small.`],[6,2,`vocabulary`,`What does anonymous mean?`,`With no name given`,[`Very famous`,`Written in verse`,`Extremely old`],`An anonymous letter is one whose writer is not named.`],[6,3,`vocabulary`,`The river was rapid after the rain. Which word could replace rapid?`,`fast`,[`slow`,`muddy`,`shallow`],`Rapid means quick or fast-moving.`],[6,3,`vocabulary`,`Precede means to come after.`,`False`,[`True`],`Precede means to come before. Proceed means to go on.`],[7,1,`vocabulary`,`What does ephemeral mean?`,`Lasting a very short time`,[`Lasting for ever`,`Very heavy`,`Full of colour`],`A soap bubble is ephemeral: it is gone in moments.`],[7,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to make something less severe or serious?`,`mitigate`,[`aggravate`,`celebrate`,`imitate`],`Planting trees can help mitigate flooding. Aggravate means the opposite: to make worse.`],[7,2,`vocabulary`,`A candid answer is…`,`honest and direct`,[`sweet and sugary`,`long and boring`,`rude and loud`],`A candid person says openly what they think.`],[7,3,`vocabulary`,`Chronology, chronic and synchronise share the root chron. What does chron mean?`,`Time`,[`Light`,`Sound`,`Water`],`Chronology is the order of events in time; synchronised clocks show the same time.`],[7,2,`vocabulary`,`Affluent means wealthy.`,`True`,[`False`],`An affluent area is one where many people have plenty of money.`],[8,1,`vocabulary`,`What does ubiquitous mean?`,`Found everywhere`,[`Very rare`,`Very ancient`,`Very loud`],`Mobile phones have become ubiquitous: you see them almost everywhere.`],[8,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to give up a right, a claim or a hold on something?`,`relinquish`,[`replenish`,`rejoice`,`refurbish`],`The king refused to relinquish his crown. Replenish means to refill.`],[8,2,`vocabulary`,`What is a euphemism?`,`A mild word used in place of a harsh or blunt one`,[`A word that sounds like the noise it names`,`A word with two opposite meanings`,`A word borrowed from another language`],`Saying someone 'passed away' instead of 'died' is a euphemism.`],[8,3,`vocabulary`,`What does pragmatic mean?`,`Practical and realistic`,[`Dreamy and unrealistic`,`Angry and loud`,`Shy and quiet`],`A pragmatic plan focuses on what will actually work.`],[8,3,`vocabulary`,`Which word describes something that happens every hundred years?`,`centennial`,[`millennial`,`annual`,`decennial`],`Centennial means every hundred years: cent- means hundred, as in century. Annual is yearly; decennial is every ten years.`],[3,1,`spelling`,`Which is the correct spelling of the day after Tuesday?`,`Wednesday`,[`Wensday`,`Wednsday`,`Wendesday`],`Wednesday has a silent d: Wed-nes-day.`],[3,2,`spelling`,`How do you spell the colour of grass?`,`green`,[`grean`,`grene`,`gren`],`Green has a double e in the middle.`],[4,1,`spelling`,`Spell the word for a young dog.`,`puppy`,[`pupy`,`puppie`,`pupyy`],`Puppy has a double p and ends in y.`],[4,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling is right for the plural of 'baby'?`,`babies`,[`babys`,`babyes`,`babis`],`When a word ends in a consonant + y, change the y to i and add -es.`],[4,3,`spelling`,`Spot the mistake: which word in 'I recieved a letter today' is misspelled?`,`recieved`,[`letter`,`today`,`I`],`After c, write ei: received, receipt, ceiling.`],[5,1,`spelling`,`How should 'acomodate' be spelled?`,`accommodate`,[`acommodate`,`accomodate`,`acomodatte`],`Accommodate has a double c and a double m: it accommodates plenty of letters!`],[5,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the shop where you buy medicines is right?`,`pharmacy`,[`farmacy`,`pharmicy`,`farmasy`],`Many words from Greek spell the f sound with ph: pharmacy, phone, photograph.`],[5,3,`spelling`,`Which of these words is spelled wrongly?`,`beleive`,[`receive`,`ceiling`,`weird`],`Beleive is the wrong spelling: it should be believe (i before e). Receive and ceiling follow 'after c', and weird is an exception.`],[5,1,`spelling`,`Library has two r's in it.`,`True`,[`False`],`Li-b-r-a-r-y: say the first r clearly so you do not forget it.`],[6,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a lake that stores water for a town is right?`,`reservoir`,[`reservior`,`resevoir`,`reservwar`],`Reservoir ends -voir, from French: res-er-voir.`],[6,2,`spelling`,`Add -ing to the word hope. Which spelling is correct?`,`hoping`,[`hopeing`,`hopping`,`hopiing`],`Drop the final e before adding -ing: hope → hoping. Hopping comes from hop.`],[6,3,`spelling`,`Which spelling shows stop with -ed added?`,`stopped`,[`stoped`,`stopt`,`stopted`],`Short vowel + one consonant: double the consonant before -ed: stop → stopped.`],[7,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for 'having a strong wish to succeed' is right?`,`ambitious`,[`ambicious`,`ambitous`,`ambishious`],`Ambitious ends -tious, like cautious and nutritious.`],[7,2,`spelling`,`Which is the right spelling of the word meaning 'certainly'?`,`definitely`,[`definately`,`definitly`,`defenitely`],`Definitely contains the word finite: de-fin-ite-ly.`],[7,3,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a feeling of awkward shame is correct?`,`embarrassment`,[`embarassment`,`embarrasment`,`embarasment`],`Embarrassment has a double r and a double s.`],[8,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the verb meaning 'to happen' is correct?`,`occur`,[`ocur`,`occurr`,`ocurr`],`Occur has a double c and a single r.`],[8,2,`spelling`,`Which is the correct past-tense spelling of occur?`,`occurred`,[`occured`,`ocurred`,`occurd`],`The stress falls on -cur, so the r is doubled before -ed: occurred.`],[8,3,`spelling`,`Which word for a spelling mistake is itself spelled correctly?`,`misspelling`,[`mispelling`,`misspeling`,`missspelling`],`Mis- + spelling = misspelling, with a double s in the middle.`]],[[3,2,`figurative`,`If a test is 'a piece of cake', it is…`,`very easy`,[`very sweet`,`very old`,`very long`],`'A piece of cake' is an idiom for something easy to do.`],[3,3,`figurative`,`Which of these phrases is a simile?`,`as brave as a lion`,[`the lion roared`,`a brave lion`,`lions are brave`],`A simile compares two things using 'like' or 'as'.`],[4,1,`figurative`,`What does it mean to 'let the cat out of the bag'?`,`To tell a secret by mistake`,[`To set a pet free`,`To go shopping`,`To lose a bag`],`The idiom has nothing to do with real cats: it means revealing a secret.`],[4,3,`figurative`,`Someone who is 'under the weather' is…`,`feeling ill`,[`standing in the rain`,`very happy`,`in a hurry`],`Feeling under the weather means feeling unwell.`],[4,2,`figurative`,`'Busy as a bee' is a simile.`,`True`,[`False`],`It compares a person to a bee using 'as', so it is a simile.`],[5,1,`figurative`,`To 'hit the books' means to…`,`study hard`,[`throw books`,`build a bookshelf`,`sell old books`],`'I have to hit the books tonight' means I must study.`],[5,2,`figurative`,`If you 'break the ice' at a party, you…`,`help people start talking`,[`spill all the drinks`,`leave the party early`,`slip on the floor`],`Breaking the ice means easing an awkward silence.`],[5,2,`figurative`,`What does the proverb 'Look before you leap' advise?`,`Think before you act`,[`Always jump as high as you can`,`Never look back`,`Run away when you are scared`],`It warns against rushing into something without thinking.`],[5,3,`figurative`,`Which idiom means to reveal a secret?`,`spill the beans`,[`hold your horses`,`cost an arm and a leg`,`once in a blue moon`],`To spill the beans is to let out information that was meant to stay secret.`],[5,3,`figurative`,`Which sentence is a metaphor?`,`The classroom was a zoo.`,[`The classroom was like a zoo.`,`The classroom was noisy.`,`The zoo had a classroom.`],`A metaphor says one thing IS another; with 'like' it would be a simile.`],[6,1,`figurative`,`Something that happens 'once in a blue moon' happens…`,`very rarely`,[`every night`,`when the Moon is full`,`only in winter`],`A 'blue moon' is an uncommon event, so the idiom means hardly ever.`],[6,2,`figurative`,`Something that 'costs an arm and a leg' is…`,`very expensive`,[`very painful`,`very cheap`,`free of charge`],`It is an exaggeration for a very high price.`],[6,2,`figurative`,`'Actions speak louder than words' means…`,`What you do shows more than what you say`,[`Shouting works better than talking`,`Quiet people are always wise`,`Words are louder than noises`],`Promises matter less than what a person actually does.`],[6,3,`figurative`,`If you have 'bitten off more than you can chew', you have…`,`taken on more than you can manage`,[`eaten far too quickly`,`talked with your mouth full`,`become very hungry`],`Agreeing to five projects at once might be biting off more than you can chew.`],[6,3,`figurative`,`In 'The wind whispered through the trees', which figure of speech is used?`,`Personification`,[`Simile`,`Onomatopoeia`,`Alliteration`],`The wind is given a human action, whispering, so it is personification.`],[7,1,`figurative`,`If a teacher tells you to 'hold your horses', you should…`,`wait and be patient`,[`go riding`,`hurry up`,`hold on tight`],`The idiom means slow down and wait.`],[7,1,`figurative`,`'Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers' is an example of…`,`Alliteration`,[`Rhyme`,`Hyperbole`,`Personification`],`Many nearby words start with the same p sound: that is alliteration.`],[7,2,`figurative`,`When a friend says 'the ball is in your court', they mean…`,`it is your turn to decide or act`,[`you have won the game`,`you have lost the ball`,`you must leave the court`],`The phrase comes from tennis: the next move is yours.`],[7,3,`figurative`,`Which proverb warns you not to rely on a result before it has actually happened?`,`Don't count your chickens before they hatch`,[`Many hands make light work`,`The early bird catches the worm`,`Practice makes perfect`],`Eggs might not all hatch, so you should not count the chicks too soon.`],[8,1,`figurative`,`Someone who 'burns the midnight oil' is…`,`working late into the night`,[`wasting electricity`,`cooking dinner late`,`starting a fire`],`The idiom comes from the days of oil lamps.`],[8,2,`figurative`,`Losing the race turned out to be 'a blessing in disguise'. What does that phrase mean?`,`A good thing that first seemed bad`,[`A present wrapped in paper`,`A secret prayer`,`A costume party`],`Missing the bus was a blessing in disguise if it meant you avoided a storm.`],[8,2,`figurative`,`What is a pun?`,`A joke that plays on different meanings or sounds of words`,[`A short poem of exactly three lines`,`A rude remark made about someone`,`A long speech given by one actor`],`'I used to be a banker, but I lost interest' is a pun.`],[8,3,`figurative`,`What does 'Still waters run deep' suggest?`,`Quiet people may have deep thoughts or feelings`,[`Rivers are deepest in winter`,`Calm water is dangerous to swim in`,`Lakes never move at all`],`The proverb says a calm surface can hide a lot underneath.`],[8,3,`figurative`,`What is a paradox?`,`A statement that seems to contradict itself but may be true`,[`A comparison using 'like' or 'as'`,`A word that copies a sound`,`A story with talking animals`],`'Less is more' is a paradox.`],[3,1,`reading`,`Who writes a book?`,`The author`,[`The reader`,`The librarian`,`The printer`],`The author writes the words; an illustrator may draw the pictures.`],[3,2,`reading`,`What does an illustrator do?`,`Draws the pictures for a book`,[`Writes the story`,`Sells the book`,`Prints the pages`],`Illustrations help to tell the story through pictures.`],[3,3,`reading`,`A story about talking animals that teaches a lesson is called a…`,`fable`,[`recipe`,`diary`,`report`],`Aesop's fables, such as 'The Tortoise and the Hare', each end with a lesson.`],[3,1,`reading`,`A dictionary lists its words in alphabetical order.`,`True`,[`False`],`Alphabetical order makes words quick to find.`],[4,1,`reading`,`Which of these is non-fiction?`,`A book about volcanoes`,[`A fairy tale`,`A comic about superheroes`,`A story about a talking cat`],`Non-fiction gives real facts and information.`],[4,2,`reading`,`What is the glossary of a book?`,`A list of special words and their meanings`,[`A list of the chapters`,`The first page of the story`,`The pictures at the end`],`Glossaries are usually at the back of information books.`],[4,3,`reading`,`What is the purpose of a recipe?`,`To instruct: it tells you how to make something`,[`To persuade you to buy food`,`To entertain you with a story`,`To describe a person`],`Instructions use numbered steps and command verbs such as mix and pour.`],[5,1,`reading`,`What is a biography?`,`The story of a person's life written by someone else`,[`A story a person writes about their own life`,`A made-up adventure story`,`A book of maps`],`A life story written by the person themselves is an autobiography.`],[5,2,`reading`,`What is the moral of a fable?`,`The lesson it teaches`,[`The name of its hero`,`The place where it happens`,`Its very last word`],`In 'The Tortoise and the Hare' the moral is 'slow and steady wins the race'.`],[5,3,`reading`,`Which kind of writing tries to make you agree with an opinion?`,`Persuasive writing`,[`A set of instructions`,`A dictionary entry`,`A weather table`],`Adverts and campaign letters are persuasive: they want you to think or act a certain way.`],[5,2,`reading`,`A haiku has three lines.`,`True`,[`False`],`A haiku is a short poem of three lines, often 5, 7 and 5 syllables.`],[6,1,`reading`,`What do we call the people or animals in a story?`,`Characters`,[`Settings`,`Chapters`,`Paragraphs`],`Characters are who the story is about.`],[6,2,`reading`,`What is a stanza?`,`A group of lines in a poem`,[`A type of rhyme`,`The title of a poem`,`A short story`],`Stanzas are to poems what paragraphs are to stories.`],[6,3,`reading`,`Which point of view tells a story using 'he', 'she' and 'they'?`,`Third person`,[`First person`,`Second person`,`Direct speech`],`A third-person narrator stands outside the story; a first-person narrator says 'I'.`],[7,1,`reading`,`What is an antagonist?`,`A character who opposes the main character`,[`The person who wrote the story`,`The place where the story is set`,`The narrator's best friend`],`In many fairy tales the wicked villain is the antagonist.`],[7,2,`reading`,`What is the resolution of a story?`,`The part where the main problem is solved`,[`The very first sentence`,`The main character's name`,`The most exciting moment`],`After the climax, the resolution ties up the story's loose ends.`],[7,3,`reading`,`What is a flashback?`,`A scene that goes back to an earlier time`,[`A flash of lightning in a story`,`A summary on the back cover`,`A scene set in the future`],`Flashbacks show events that happened before the main story.`],[7,2,`reading`,`An autobiography is written by someone about their own life.`,`True`,[`False`],`Auto- means self: an autobiography is a self-written life story.`],[8,1,`reading`,`What is dialogue in a story?`,`The words characters say to each other`,[`The description of the setting`,`The list of chapters`,`The author's biography`],`Dialogue is usually set out in speech marks with a new line for each speaker.`],[8,2,`reading`,`What is bias in a piece of writing?`,`Unfairly favouring one side`,[`Using long words`,`Writing in the past tense`,`Including a diagram`],`Biased writing leaves out facts that do not suit its point of view.`],[8,2,`reading`,`What is free verse?`,`Poetry without a regular rhyme or rhythm`,[`Poetry you do not have to pay for`,`A poem with exactly 14 lines`,`A poem that always rhymes`],`Free verse follows the natural rhythms of speech.`],[8,3,`reading`,`Which is the strongest evidence for a claim in an argument?`,`A fact from a reliable source`,[`A rumour from a friend`,`The writer's own guess`,`A catchy slogan`],`Good arguments are backed by checkable evidence, not hearsay.`]],[[3,1,`grammar`,`Which word in ‘The happy baby laughed’ tells you what the baby was like?`,`happy`,[`laughed`,`baby`,`The`],`Happy is an adjective: it describes the baby.`],[3,1,`grammar`,`Which of these words is a naming word (a noun)?`,`table`,[`run`,`blue`,`slowly`],`A noun names a person, place or thing. A table is a thing.`],[3,2,`grammar`,`Which word fills the gap in ‘Ben and Mia ___ my friends’?`,`are`,[`is`,`am`,`be`],`Two people make a plural subject, so the verb is ‘are’.`],[3,2,`grammar`,`Which word joins the two ideas in ‘I was hungry so I ate a banana’?`,`so`,[`hungry`,`ate`,`was`],`‘So’ is a conjunction that links the cause (hunger) to the result (eating).`],[3,3,`grammar`,`One mouse, two …`,`mice`,[`mouses`,`mices`,`meese`],`Mouse has an irregular plural: mice.`],[3,3,`grammar`,`The word ‘and’ is used to join words or ideas together.`,`True`,[`False`],`‘And’ is a conjunction, as in ‘bread and butter’.`],[4,1,`grammar`,`Yesterday we ___ in the river. Which form of the verb ‘swim’ fits?`,`swam`,[`swum`,`swimmed`,`swims`],`Swim is irregular: today I swim, yesterday I swam, I have swum.`],[4,1,`grammar`,`Only one of these sentences uses the word an properly. Which one?`,`She ate an orange.`,[`She ate an banana.`,`He saw an dog.`,`We need an pencil.`],`‘An’ comes before a vowel sound, as in ‘an orange’; ‘a’ comes before a consonant sound.`],[4,2,`grammar`,`‘The keys are under the sofa.’ Which word tells you where the keys are?`,`under`,[`keys`,`sofa`,`are`],`‘Under’ is a preposition: it shows the position of the keys.`],[4,2,`grammar`,`Which of these nouns is plural?`,`geese`,[`goose`,`mouse`,`tooth`],`Geese is the plural of goose; the others are singular.`],[4,2,`grammar`,`My cold is ___ today than it was yesterday. Which form of ‘bad’ fits?`,`worse`,[`badder`,`worst`,`more bad`],`Bad is irregular: bad, worse, worst. Comparing two days needs ‘worse’.`],[4,3,`grammar`,`In which sentence is too the right word?`,`It is too hot to play outside.`,[`I have too apples.`,`She went too the shop.`,`We walked too the river.`],`‘Too’ means ‘more than enough’ or ‘also’; ‘to’ shows direction and ‘two’ is the number.`],[4,3,`grammar`,`A proper noun names one particular person, place or thing, and starts with a capital letter.`,`True`,[`False`],`Dhaka, Rahim and Friday are proper nouns; city, boy and day are common nouns.`],[5,1,`grammar`,`In which sentence is their the right spelling?`,`The children packed their bags.`,[`Their is a cat outside.`,`The bags are over their.`,`Their going home now.`],`‘Their’ means ‘belonging to them’. ‘There’ is a place and ‘they're’ means ‘they are’.`],[5,1,`grammar`,`Which word is an adverb of time?`,`yesterday`,[`slowly`,`here`,`very`],`‘Yesterday’ tells you when. ‘Slowly’ tells how and ‘here’ tells where.`],[5,2,`grammar`,`In ‘Although it rained, we played outside’, which part is the main clause?`,`we played outside`,[`Although it rained`,`it rained`,`Although`],`‘We played outside’ makes sense on its own; ‘although it rained’ depends on it.`],[5,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence is in the present progressive (continuous) tense?`,`She is reading a book.`,[`She reads a book.`,`She read a book.`,`She will read a book.`],`‘Is reading’ (am/is/are + -ing) shows an action happening now.`],[5,3,`grammar`,`Which pair contains two prepositions?`,`under, between`,[`quickly, under`,`between, happy`,`run, over`],`Both ‘under’ and ‘between’ show position. ‘Quickly’ is an adverb, ‘happy’ an adjective and ‘run’ a verb.`],[5,3,`grammar`,`A noun can be the subject of a sentence.`,`True`,[`False`],`In ‘The river flooded’, the noun ‘river’ is the subject.`],[6,1,`grammar`,`Which sentence uses the past perfect tense?`,`They had left before we arrived.`,[`They left before we arrived.`,`They have left already.`,`They are leaving soon.`],`Had + past participle (had left) shows an action finished before another past action.`],[6,1,`grammar`,`How many pronouns are in ‘She gave it to them’?`,`3`,[`1`,`2`,`4`],`She, it and them are all pronouns.`],[6,2,`grammar`,`In ‘Mum gave me a present’, which word is the indirect object?`,`me`,[`present`,`Mum`,`gave`],`The present is what was given (direct object); ‘me’ is who received it (indirect object).`],[6,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence joins two main clauses with a coordinating conjunction?`,`I was tired, so I went to bed.`,[`When I was tired, I slept.`,`Because I was tired, I slept.`,`Tired, I went to bed.`],`‘So’ is a coordinating conjunction (like and, but, or) joining two clauses of equal weight.`],[6,3,`grammar`,`Which verb agrees with the subject in ‘Each of the boys ___ a bag’?`,`has`,[`have`,`are having`,`were having`],`‘Each’ is singular, so it takes the singular verb ‘has’.`],[6,3,`grammar`,`In ‘The cake was eaten by the children’, the children did the eating.`,`True`,[`False`],`The sentence is passive: the cake receives the action, and the children are the ones doing it.`],[7,1,`grammar`,`In ‘You must wear a helmet’, what does ‘must’ express?`,`Obligation`,[`Possibility`,`Ability`,`A question`],`‘Must’ shows obligation (something is required); ‘might’ shows possibility and ‘can’ ability.`],[7,1,`grammar`,`‘I am tired,’ said Mum. Which is the correct reported speech?`,`Mum said that she was tired.`,[`Mum said that I was tired.`,`Mum said that she will be tired.`,`Mum said, that she tired.`],`In reported speech ‘I’ becomes ‘she’ and ‘am’ moves back to ‘was’.`],[7,2,`grammar`,`What is an infinitive?`,`The base form of a verb with ‘to’, such as ‘to run’`,[`A verb form that ends in -ing`,`A word that joins two clauses`,`A verb in the past tense`],`‘To swim’, ‘to read’ and ‘to be’ are infinitives.`],[7,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence is a conditional (an ‘if’ sentence about a possible result)?`,`If it rains, we will stay inside.`,[`It rains, so we stay inside.`,`We stayed inside because it rained.`,`Rain kept us inside.`],`A conditional has an ‘if’ clause (the condition) and a result clause.`],[7,3,`grammar`,`An adjective can come after a verb such as ‘is’, as in ‘The sky is blue’.`,`True`,[`False`],`Here ‘blue’ follows the linking verb ‘is’ and still describes the sky.`],[8,1,`grammar`,`Which sentence contains a split infinitive?`,`She wanted to quickly finish.`,[`She wanted to finish quickly.`,`Quickly, she finished.`,`She finished it quickly.`],`The adverb ‘quickly’ splits ‘to’ from ‘finish’.`],[8,1,`grammar`,`Which word is a subordinating conjunction?`,`although`,[`and`,`but`,`or`],`‘Although’ begins a subordinate clause; and, but and or are coordinating conjunctions.`],[8,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence uses the passive voice to avoid saying who did something?`,`Mistakes were made.`,[`I made mistakes.`,`We made some mistakes.`,`The team made mistakes.`],`The passive lets a writer leave out the doer, so nobody is named.`],[8,2,`grammar`,`In ‘The book, which I bought yesterday, is excellent’, the clause ‘which I bought yesterday’ is…`,`non-defining (extra information)`,[`defining (essential information)`,`the main clause`,`a prepositional phrase`],`It adds extra detail and is set off by commas; the sentence still works without it.`],[8,3,`grammar`,`‘Data’ was originally a plural word; its singular is ‘datum’.`,`True`,[`False`],`It comes from Latin. Many people now also use ‘data’ as a singular mass noun.`],[3,1,`vocabulary`,`The puppy was tiny. Which word means the same as tiny?`,`small`,[`huge`,`loud`,`fast`],`Tiny means very small.`],[3,1,`vocabulary`,`If someone asks you to shut the door, what should you do?`,`Close it`,[`Open it`,`Paint it`,`Knock on it`],`Shut means close: shutting the door closes it.`],[3,2,`vocabulary`,`The library was quiet, but the playground was ___.`,`noisy`,[`silent`,`calm`,`empty`],`The word ‘but’ signals a contrast, so the opposite of quiet fits: noisy.`],[3,2,`vocabulary`,`What do we call a person who flies an aeroplane?`,`A pilot`,[`A driver`,`A sailor`,`A farmer`],`A pilot flies aircraft; a sailor works on ships.`],[3,3,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to speak very quietly?`,`whisper`,[`shout`,`sing`,`cry`],`To whisper is to speak softly, using breath rather than full voice.`],[3,3,`vocabulary`,`A chef is a person who cooks in a restaurant.`,`True`,[`False`],`Chef comes from French and means the head cook.`],[4,1,`vocabulary`,`What do you call a large group of fish swimming together?`,`A shoal`,[`A herd`,`A flock`,`A pack`],`Fish swim in shoals (or schools); cows are in herds and birds in flocks.`],[4,1,`vocabulary`,`If a room is spotless, it is…`,`completely clean`,[`full of spots`,`very dark`,`very small`],`Spotless means without a single spot of dirt.`],[4,2,`vocabulary`,`What does the prefix mis- add to words such as misbehave and misspell?`,`Badly or wrongly`,[`Again`,`Before`,`Very much`],`To misbehave is to behave badly; to misspell is to spell wrongly.`],[4,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word does NOT mean happy?`,`miserable`,[`cheerful`,`glad`,`delighted`],`Miserable means very unhappy; the other three are synonyms of happy.`],[4,3,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to look at something for a long time?`,`stare`,[`glance`,`blink`,`peek`],`A glance or a peek is quick; a stare is long and fixed.`],[4,3,`vocabulary`,`Gigantic and minute (pronounced my-NEWT, meaning tiny) are antonyms.`,`True`,[`False`],`Gigantic means huge and minute means tiny, so they have opposite meanings.`],[5,1,`vocabulary`,`What does the suffix -less mean in fearless and careless?`,`without`,[`full of`,`again`,`before`],`Fearless means without fear; -ful means full of.`],[5,1,`vocabulary`,`A courageous firefighter is…`,`brave`,[`scared`,`tired`,`careless`],`Courageous means showing courage, which is bravery.`],[5,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to keep putting off something you should do?`,`procrastinate`,[`hurry`,`celebrate`,`organise`],`To procrastinate is to delay doing something, often until the last minute.`],[5,2,`vocabulary`,`What does vacant mean in ‘a vacant seat on the bus’?`,`Empty`,[`Broken`,`Expensive`,`Reserved`],`A vacant seat has nobody in it.`],[5,3,`vocabulary`,`The word biology contains bio. What does bio mean?`,`Life`,[`Book`,`Write`,`Two`],`Biology is the study of life; a biography is the story of a life.`],[5,3,`vocabulary`,`Annual means happening once a year.`,`True`,[`False`],`An annual sports day is held every year; it comes from Latin annus, a year.`],[6,1,`vocabulary`,`Which word means happening at the same time?`,`simultaneous`,[`consecutive`,`previous`,`eventual`],`Consecutive things come one after another; simultaneous things happen together.`],[6,1,`vocabulary`,`Words such as autograph and paragraph contain the Greek root graph. What does it mean?`,`Write`,[`Hear`,`See`,`Light`],`An autograph is a person's own written name; a paragraph is a piece of writing.`],[6,2,`vocabulary`,`The Latin root aqua, as in aquarium and aquatic, means…`,`water`,[`air`,`fish`,`glass`],`An aquarium holds water creatures, and aquatic plants grow in water.`],[6,2,`vocabulary`,`A novice is someone who is…`,`new to an activity`,[`an expert at it`,`very old`,`a writer of novels`],`A novice is a beginner with little experience.`],[6,3,`vocabulary`,`Which word means a short journey made for pleasure?`,`An excursion`,[`An expedition`,`A migration`,`A commute`],`An excursion is a short trip for pleasure, like a school day out; an expedition is a long, organised journey with a purpose.`],[6,3,`vocabulary`,`Optimistic means expecting things to turn out badly.`,`False`,[`True`],`Optimistic means hopeful; expecting the worst is being pessimistic.`],[7,1,`vocabulary`,`An eloquent speaker is one who speaks…`,`fluently and persuasively`,[`very loudly and fast`,`shyly and quietly`,`rudely and unkindly`],`Eloquent speech is clear, expressive and moving.`],[7,1,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to make something seem less important than it really is?`,`downplay`,[`exaggerate`,`emphasise`,`celebrate`],`To downplay is the opposite of to exaggerate.`],[7,2,`vocabulary`,`To scrutinise a document is to…`,`examine it very closely`,[`throw it away quickly`,`praise it loudly`,`copy it out by hand`],`Scrutiny is careful, detailed inspection.`],[7,2,`vocabulary`,`Philanthropist comes from Greek phil (love) and anthropos (human). A philanthropist…`,`helps others, often by giving money`,[`dislikes other people`,`studies plants and trees`,`collects old stamps`],`A philanthropist shows love of people through generous giving.`],[7,3,`vocabulary`,`The Latin root port, as in transport and portable, means…`,`carry`,[`build`,`write`,`sleep`],`Transport carries things across; something portable can be carried.`],[7,3,`vocabulary`,`Inevitable means certain to happen.`,`True`,[`False`],`Something inevitable cannot be avoided.`],[8,1,`vocabulary`,`A gregarious person is…`,`sociable and fond of company`,[`greedy and selfish`,`shy and quiet`,`easily angered`],`Gregarious comes from Latin grex, a flock: such people like being with others.`],[8,1,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to calm an angry person by doing something they want?`,`placate`,[`provoke`,`irritate`,`abandon`],`To placate is to soothe; to provoke is to make angrier.`],[8,2,`vocabulary`,`Obsolete technology is…`,`out of date and no longer used`,[`brand new and expensive`,`very large and heavy`,`hard to find in shops`],`Floppy discs are obsolete: they have been replaced by newer technology.`],[8,2,`vocabulary`,`What is a person who comes to live permanently in a new country called?`,`An immigrant`,[`An emigrant`,`A tourist`,`A native`],`An immigrant arrives in a new country; an emigrant is the same person seen as leaving their old one.`],[8,3,`vocabulary`,`A loquacious person…`,`talks a great deal`,[`eats a great deal`,`sleeps a great deal`,`travels a great deal`],`Loquacious comes from Latin loqui, to speak.`],[8,3,`vocabulary`,`Ambivalent means having mixed feelings about something.`,`True`,[`False`],`Ambi- means both: an ambivalent person feels two ways at once.`],[3,1,`spelling`,`Which is the correct spelling of the number after eleven?`,`twelve`,[`twelf`,`twelv`,`tweleve`],`Twelve ends with a silent e after the v.`],[3,1,`spelling`,`How do you spell the name of the season after summer?`,`autumn`,[`autum`,`awtumn`,`autumm`],`Autumn ends with a silent n.`],[3,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the bird that hoots at night is right?`,`owl`,[`oul`,`owel`,`aul`],`Owl is spelled o-w-l.`],[3,2,`spelling`,`Which letters complete sch _ _ l, the place where children learn?`,`oo`,[`ue`,`ou`,`ol`],`School is spelled s-c-h-o-o-l.`],[3,3,`spelling`,`Becuase is the right way to spell because.`,`False`,[`True`],`The correct spelling is because: b-e-c-a-u-s-e.`],[4,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the plural of leaf is correct?`,`leaves`,[`leafs`,`leafes`,`leavs`],`Many words ending in f change to -ves in the plural: leaf, leaves.`],[4,1,`spelling`,`Add -ed to carry. Which spelling is correct?`,`carried`,[`carryed`,`caried`,`carred`],`After a consonant, y changes to i before -ed: carry, carried.`],[4,2,`spelling`,`Which word has a silent w?`,`write`,[`water`,`window`,`twin`],`In write the w is not pronounced; in the others it is heard.`],[4,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the month after January is correct?`,`February`,[`Febuary`,`Febraury`,`Februery`],`February has an r after the b, which many people forget to say.`],[4,3,`spelling`,`How is happy + -ness spelled?`,`happiness`,[`happyness`,`hapiness`,`happines`],`The y changes to i: happy becomes happiness.`],[5,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the plural of potato is correct?`,`potatoes`,[`potatos`,`potatoe`,`potatose`],`Potato and tomato add -es in the plural.`],[5,1,`spelling`,`Which word follows the rule ‘i before e, except after c’?`,`receive`,[`recieve`,`beleive`,`peice`],`After c the order is e then i: receive. The others are misspellings.`],[5,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the room where scientists do experiments is correct?`,`laboratory`,[`labratory`,`laboratary`,`labortory`],`Laboratory has five syllables: la-bor-a-tor-y.`],[5,2,`spelling`,`In British English, which spelling is used for a building where plays are performed?`,`theatre`,[`theater`,`theatere`,`theetre`],`British English writes theatre; American English writes theater.`],[5,3,`spelling`,`Which is the correct spelling of the word for everything you know and understand?`,`knowledge`,[`knowlege`,`nowledge`,`knowladge`],`Knowledge has a silent k and ends in -dge.`],[6,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a person you work with is correct?`,`colleague`,[`collegue`,`colleage`,`coleague`],`Colleague has a double l and ends in -ague.`],[6,1,`spelling`,`Which is the correct spelling of the word meaning to suggest something as good?`,`recommend`,[`reccomend`,`recomend`,`reccommend`],`Recommend has one c and a double m.`],[6,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning at once, without delay, is right?`,`immediately`,[`immediatly`,`imediately`,`immediatley`],`It is immediate + -ly, with a double m.`],[6,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for the natural world around us is correct?`,`environment`,[`enviroment`,`envirnoment`,`environmant`],`Environment has an n before the m: environ-ment.`],[6,3,`spelling`,`Words ending in a consonant and y change the y to i before adding -es, as in city → cities.`,`True`,[`False`],`So baby becomes babies, but a vowel before the y keeps it: day, days.`],[7,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a firm promise is correct?`,`guarantee`,[`garantee`,`guarentee`,`guarantie`],`Guarantee has a silent u after the g.`],[7,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a word with the opposite meaning is right?`,`antonym`,[`antonim`,`anntonym`,`antanym`],`Antonym comes from Greek anti (against) and onyma (name).`],[7,2,`spelling`,`Which is the correct British spelling of the word for someone who lives next door?`,`neighbour`,[`nieghbour`,`neihbour`,`naybour`],`Neighbour follows ‘e before i’ in the ‘ay’ sound, like weigh and eight.`],[7,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning awake and aware is correct?`,`conscious`,[`concious`,`consious`,`conshus`],`Conscious contains the letters sc before -ious.`],[7,3,`spelling`,`The word rhythm contains none of the vowels a, e, i, o or u.`,`True`,[`False`],`Rhythm uses y as its vowel sound: r-h-y-t-h-m.`],[8,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning extremely tired is correct?`,`exhausted`,[`exausted`,`exhaustid`,`exhosted`],`Exhausted has a silent h after the x.`],[8,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a person who starts a business is correct?`,`entrepreneur`,[`entrepeneur`,`enterpreneur`,`entreprenuer`],`It comes from French: entre-pre-neur.`],[8,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a fixed time to see a doctor is correct?`,`appointment`,[`apointment`,`appointmant`,`apoyntment`],`Appointment has a double p: ap-point-ment.`],[8,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning careful and hard-working is correct?`,`conscientious`,[`conscientous`,`consciencious`,`contientious`],`Conscientious comes from conscience: con-sci-en-tious.`],[8,3,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning ‘to put up with something unpleasant’ is correct?`,`tolerate`,[`tollerate`,`tolarate`,`tolerait`],`Tolerate has one l and an e after it: tol-er-ate.`]],[[3,1,`punctuation`,`Which person's name is written correctly?`,`Ruma Akter`,[`ruma akter`,`Ruma akter`,`ruma Akter`],`Both parts of a person's name are proper nouns, so each starts with a capital letter.`],[3,2,`punctuation`,`What does a full stop show?`,`The end of a sentence`,[`That a question is asked`,`A strong feeling`,`That someone is speaking`],`A full stop marks where a statement ends.`],[3,3,`punctuation`,`The word I is always written as a capital letter when it means yourself.`,`True`,[`False`],`‘I’ on its own, meaning the speaker, is always a capital: ‘Can I come?’`],[4,1,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses an apostrophe to show that letters are missing?`,`It's raining.`,[`The cat's bowl is empty.`,`Tom's book is red.`,`The girls' bags are heavy.`],`It's is short for ‘it is’; the apostrophe stands in for the missing i. The others show belonging.`],[4,1,`punctuation`,`Which contraction means ‘they are’?`,`they're`,[`their`,`there`,`theyr`],`They're = they + are, with the apostrophe replacing the a.`],[4,2,`punctuation`,`Where does the comma go in ‘Yes I would like some cake’?`,`After Yes`,[`After would`,`After like`,`After some`],`A comma follows an opening word such as yes or no: ‘Yes, I would like some cake.’`],[4,2,`punctuation`,`Speech marks (inverted commas) go around…`,`the words someone actually says`,[`the name of the speaker`,`every noun in the sentence`,`the whole paragraph`],`Only the spoken words go inside: “Look out!” shouted Raj.`],[4,3,`punctuation`,`An exclamation mark can show surprise, excitement or a loud voice.`,`True`,[`False`],`For example: ‘What a huge cake!’ or ‘Stop!’`],[5,1,`punctuation`,`Which sentence shows that one girl owns the bike?`,`The girl's bike is red.`,[`The girls bike is red.`,`The girls' bike is red.`,`The girl,s bike is red.`],`For one owner, add apostrophe + s: the girl's. The girls' would mean several girls.`],[5,2,`punctuation`,`‘Can you help me’ asked Sam. Where should the question mark go?`,`Inside the speech marks, after me`,[`After Sam, at the very end`,`After asked, before Sam`,`Before Can, at the start`],`The question is what Sam says, so the question mark goes inside: “Can you help me?” asked Sam.`],[5,3,`punctuation`,`In a story, a new paragraph should start when a new person speaks.`,`True`,[`False`],`Starting a new line for each speaker makes a conversation easy to follow.`],[6,1,`punctuation`,`What does a list of bullet points do in a piece of writing?`,`Sets out separate points clearly`,[`Shows that someone is speaking`,`Marks a question`,`Joins two sentences into one`],`Bullet points break information into short, separate items that are easy to scan.`],[6,2,`punctuation`,`In ‘the students' books’, how many students own the books?`,`More than one`,[`Exactly one`,`None`,`It cannot be told`],`The apostrophe after the s shows a plural owner: several students.`],[6,3,`punctuation`,`A colon must always be followed by a capital letter.`,`False`,[`True`],`After a colon you usually carry on in lower case: ‘Bring three things: a pen, a ruler and a book.’`],[7,1,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses its and it's correctly?`,`It's time the dog had its dinner.`,[`Its time the dog had it's dinner.`,`It's time the dog had it's dinner.`,`Its time the dog had its dinner.`],`It's means ‘it is’ (it is time); its means ‘belonging to it’ (its dinner).`],[7,2,`punctuation`,`Which punctuation mark can join two closely related main clauses without ‘and’?`,`A semicolon`,[`A comma on its own`,`An apostrophe`,`A question mark`],`‘It was late; we went home.’ A comma alone there would be a comma splice.`],[7,2,`punctuation`,`Which sentence is a comma splice (an error)?`,`It was late, we went home.`,[`It was late, so we went home.`,`It was late; we went home.`,`Because it was late, we went home.`],`Two main clauses joined only by a comma make a comma splice; add a conjunction or use a semicolon.`],[7,3,`punctuation`,`Single and double quotation marks can both show speech, as long as you use them consistently.`,`True`,[`False`],`British books often use single marks and American books double; either is fine if consistent.`],[8,1,`punctuation`,`In a quotation, what are square brackets [ ] used for?`,`Adding words that were not in the original`,[`Showing who is speaking`,`Listing items`,`Ending the sentence`],`‘She [the queen] waved’ adds words to make a quotation clear.`],[8,2,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses a comma to keep Grandma safe?`,`Let's eat, Grandma!`,[`Let's eat Grandma!`,`Lets eat Grandma!`,`Let's, eat Grandma!`],`With the comma, Grandma is being spoken to; without it, she is what is being eaten.`],[8,3,`punctuation`,`What does an apostrophe never do in standard English?`,`Make an ordinary plural, as in apple's for sale`,[`Show that letters are missing`,`Show belonging`,`Form contractions like won't`],`Plurals take no apostrophe: apples for sale. The mistake is often called the greengrocer's apostrophe.`],[3,1,`figurative`,`If you are all ears, you are…`,`listening carefully`,[`very hungry`,`feeling sleepy`,`making a noise`],`‘All ears’ means paying full attention to what is being said.`],[3,2,`figurative`,`What does it mean when people say it is raining cats and dogs?`,`It is raining very heavily`,[`Animals are falling from the sky`,`There is a light drizzle`,`The sun is shining`],`It is an idiom for very heavy rain.`],[3,3,`figurative`,`‘The sun smiled down on us’ gives the sun a human action.`,`True`,[`False`],`The sun cannot really smile; giving it a human action is personification.`],[4,1,`figurative`,`What does over the moon mean?`,`Very happy`,[`Very far away`,`Lost in space`,`Very tired`],`If you are over the moon, you are delighted.`],[4,1,`figurative`,`Which word imitates the sound a snake makes?`,`hiss`,[`bark`,`roar`,`tweet`],`Hiss sounds like what it names, which is onomatopoeia.`],[4,2,`figurative`,`If someone gets cold feet about a plan, they…`,`feel nervous and want to back out`,[`have very chilly toes`,`feel brave and ready`,`are excited to start`],`Cold feet means losing courage just before doing something.`],[4,2,`figurative`,`‘It's not rocket science’ means that something is…`,`not very difficult`,[`about space travel`,`very dangerous`,`a secret`],`It is said about a task that is easy to understand.`],[4,3,`figurative`,`Calling someone a night owl means they like to stay up late.`,`True`,[`False`],`Owls are active at night, so a night owl is a person who prefers late hours.`],[5,1,`figurative`,`If you bite your tongue, you…`,`stop yourself from saying something`,[`hurt your mouth while eating`,`eat your food too fast`,`shout out an answer`],`The idiom means holding back words you would like to say.`],[5,1,`figurative`,`What does the saying ‘the early bird catches the worm’ mean?`,`People who start early often succeed`,[`Birds eat worms in the morning`,`Never wake up too early`,`Worms are hard to find`],`Acting early gives you the best chance.`],[5,2,`figurative`,`If a machine is on its last legs, it…`,`is about to stop working`,[`is brand new`,`has many legs`,`is running very fast`],`‘On its last legs’ means worn out and close to failing.`],[5,2,`figurative`,`To hit the nail on the head means to…`,`say exactly the right thing`,[`hurt your thumb with a hammer`,`build a wooden box`,`lose your temper`],`It means describing something precisely.`],[5,3,`figurative`,`If you pull someone's leg, you…`,`tease them in fun`,[`help them stand up`,`hurt them on purpose`,`ignore them`],`Pulling someone's leg is joking with them, often by saying something untrue.`],[6,1,`figurative`,`If something is the last straw, it is…`,`the final problem that makes you give up`,[`the last drink at a party`,`a piece of farm rubbish`,`the easiest part of a job`],`It comes from ‘the straw that broke the camel's back’.`],[6,2,`figurative`,`People say every cloud has a silver lining. What do they mean?`,`Something good can come from a bad situation`,[`Clouds are made of silver`,`It will always rain`,`Bad luck never ends`],`Even troubles can have a hopeful side.`],[6,2,`figurative`,`‘A sea of faces’ is an example of…`,`a metaphor`,[`a simile`,`onomatopoeia`,`alliteration`],`The crowd is called a sea without ‘like’ or ‘as’, so it is a metaphor.`],[6,3,`figurative`,`The meaning of an idiom can usually be worked out from the meanings of its separate words.`,`False`,[`True`],`Idioms such as ‘spill the beans’ mean more than their words: here, to tell a secret.`],[7,1,`figurative`,`‘Don't put all your eggs in one basket’ advises you to…`,`spread your risks`,[`carry eggs carefully`,`buy more baskets`,`eat eggs every day`],`If everything depends on one thing, you could lose it all at once.`],[7,1,`figurative`,`To turn over a new leaf means to…`,`start behaving better`,[`do some gardening`,`read a new book`,`sweep up leaves`],`It means making a fresh start.`],[7,2,`figurative`,`Which line is the clearest example of assonance (repeated vowel sounds)?`,`The rain in Spain stays mainly on the plain.`,[`Big brown bears bounce boldly.`,`Crash! Bang! Wallop!`,`Her smile was sunshine.`],`The long ‘ay’ sound repeats in rain, Spain, stays, mainly and plain.`],[7,2,`figurative`,`In a mystery story, what is a red herring?`,`A false clue that misleads`,[`A fish served for dinner`,`The real culprit`,`The final chapter`],`Red herrings send readers and detectives the wrong way.`],[7,3,`figurative`,`A simile always compares using the word like or as.`,`True`,[`False`],`‘As fast as a cheetah’ and ‘swam like a fish’ are similes.`],[8,1,`figurative`,`What is an extended metaphor?`,`A metaphor developed over several lines or a whole text`,[`A very long simile`,`A list of different metaphors`,`A metaphor that rhymes`],`For example, a whole poem may describe life as a journey.`],[8,1,`figurative`,`Stormy weather that matches a character's angry mood is an example of…`,`pathetic fallacy`,[`onomatopoeia`,`an oxymoron`,`a pun`],`Pathetic fallacy gives nature human feelings that reflect the mood of the story.`],[8,2,`figurative`,`What is someone's Achilles' heel?`,`A small but serious weakness`,[`Their greatest strength`,`A running injury`,`A kind of shoe`],`In Greek myth, Achilles could only be wounded in his heel.`],[8,2,`figurative`,`To cross the Rubicon means to…`,`take a step that cannot be undone`,[`go for a swim in a river`,`travel all the way to Rome`,`refuse to make any decision`],`Julius Caesar crossed the river Rubicon with his army in 49 BC, starting a civil war.`],[8,3,`figurative`,`Understatement is the opposite of hyperbole.`,`True`,[`False`],`Hyperbole exaggerates; understatement makes something sound smaller than it is: ‘It's a bit wet’ during a flood.`],[3,1,`reading`,`What is the title of a book?`,`Its name`,[`Its first page`,`The person who wrote it`,`Its pictures`],`The title is the name of the book, shown on the cover.`],[3,1,`reading`,`Where would you look to find what a word means?`,`In a dictionary`,[`In an atlas`,`In a calendar`,`In a menu`],`A dictionary gives the meanings of words.`],[3,2,`reading`,`What is a fairy tale often like?`,`A story with magic, often with a happy ending`,[`A list of true facts about animals`,`A set of numbered instructions`,`A report on today's news`],`Fairy tales usually feature magic, such as talking animals or spells.`],[3,3,`reading`,`An atlas is a book of maps.`,`True`,[`False`],`An atlas collects maps of countries, continents and the world.`],[4,1,`reading`,`What is the blurb of a book?`,`A short description on the back cover`,[`The first chapter`,`The list of chapters`,`The index`],`The blurb tells you a little about the book to make you want to read it.`],[4,2,`reading`,`What is the index at the back of a book for?`,`Finding the pages where topics appear`,[`Reading the whole story`,`Drawing your own pictures`,`Saving your place`],`The index lists topics in alphabetical order with their page numbers.`],[4,2,`reading`,`How does a friendly letter usually begin?`,`With a greeting such as ‘Dear Sam’`,[`With the writer's signature`,`With the address of a shop`,`With a question`],`A letter starts by greeting the person it is written to.`],[4,3,`reading`,`Instructions are often written as numbered steps.`,`True`,[`False`],`Numbered steps make the order clear, as in a recipe.`],[5,1,`reading`,`What is a cliffhanger at the end of a chapter?`,`An exciting moment left unresolved`,[`A happy ending`,`A summary of the story`,`A list of characters`],`Cliffhangers keep readers wanting to find out what happens next.`],[5,1,`reading`,`What is a headline in a newspaper?`,`The title of a news story in large print`,[`The name of the reporter`,`The last line of a story`,`A photograph`],`Headlines sum up a story quickly to catch the reader's eye.`],[5,2,`reading`,`What does skimming a text mean?`,`Reading quickly to get the general idea`,[`Reading every word slowly`,`Copying it out by hand`,`Reading it aloud to a class`],`Skimming gives an overview; you look for headings and key sentences.`],[5,2,`reading`,`In a diary entry, the writer usually writes…`,`in the first person about their own day`,[`in the third person about strangers`,`as a poem with rhymes`,`as a list of rules`],`Diaries use ‘I’ and describe the writer's own thoughts and events.`],[5,3,`reading`,`What is the list of characters at the start of a playscript called?`,`The cast list`,[`The chorus`,`The index`,`The blurb`],`The cast list names everyone who appears in the play.`],[5,3,`reading`,`Stage directions in a play tell actors what to do, not what to say.`,`True`,[`False`],`Stage directions, often in brackets or italics, describe actions, movements and settings.`],[6,1,`reading`,`What does scanning a text mean?`,`Searching quickly for one particular detail`,[`Reading every word twice over`,`Summarising the whole text`,`Writing notes in the margins`],`You scan a timetable for your train, or a page for a name or date.`],[6,1,`reading`,`Who is the narrator of a story?`,`The voice that tells the story`,[`The writer's editor`,`The main villain`,`The illustrator`],`The narrator may be a character (‘I’) or an outside voice.`],[6,2,`reading`,`What is a myth?`,`A traditional story about gods or heroes`,[`A true report of recent events`,`A set of rules for a game`,`A short funny poem`],`Many myths explain natural events or how the world began.`],[6,2,`reading`,`What is a topic sentence?`,`A sentence that introduces the main idea of a paragraph`,[`The last sentence of a book`,`A sentence with no verb`,`A sentence that asks a question`],`Readers can often find the point of a paragraph from its topic sentence.`],[6,3,`reading`,`A fact can be checked and shown to be true; an opinion is what someone thinks or feels.`,`True`,[`False`],`‘Dhaka is in Bangladesh’ is a fact; ‘Dhaka is the best city’ is an opinion.`],[7,1,`reading`,`What is the tone of a piece of writing?`,`The writer's attitude, shown by word choice`,[`The number of pages it has`,`The size of the printed letters`,`The title on the front cover`],`Tone can be serious, playful, angry, sad and so on.`],[7,1,`reading`,`What is a sub-plot?`,`A smaller story running alongside the main one`,[`The main plot of a story`,`The final chapter`,`A map of the setting`],`Sub-plots add depth, often involving minor characters.`],[7,2,`reading`,`What is an unreliable narrator?`,`A narrator whose account cannot be fully trusted`,[`A narrator who is always late`,`A narrator who speaks in rhyme`,`A narrator who is a robot`],`They may be lying, mistaken or only seeing part of the truth.`],[7,2,`reading`,`What is imagery in writing?`,`Language that appeals to the senses to create pictures`,[`Pictures printed in a book`,`A list of facts and figures`,`Writing in very large letters`],`‘The sour smell of rotting apples’ is imagery: you can almost smell it.`],[7,3,`reading`,`A ballad is a poem that tells a story, often in short verses.`,`True`,[`False`],`Ballads were often sung and passed on by word of mouth.`],[8,1,`reading`,`What is dramatic irony?`,`When the audience knows something a character does not`,[`When a play is very dramatic`,`When a joke falls flat`,`When the ending is happy`],`Watching a character walk into a trap we know about creates tension.`],[8,1,`reading`,`What is a soliloquy in a play?`,`A speech in which a character speaks their thoughts alone`,[`A song sung by the whole cast`,`A conversation between two characters`,`The list of scenes`],`Hamlet's ‘To be, or not to be’ is a famous soliloquy.`],[8,2,`reading`,`What does genre mean?`,`A type of writing, such as horror or romance`,[`The length of a book`,`The name of the publisher`,`The price of a book`],`Genres group texts with similar features: science fiction, mystery, fantasy…`],[8,2,`reading`,`In an argument essay, what is a counter-argument?`,`An opposing view that the writer then answers`,[`The writer's first main point`,`A list of sources`,`The title of the essay`],`Dealing with the other side makes an argument more convincing.`],[8,3,`reading`,`What is the pace of a story?`,`How quickly the events seem to move`,[`The number of chapters`,`The age of the main character`,`The place where it is set`],`Short sentences and quick action speed the pace up; long description slows it down.`],[8,3,`reading`,`An epilogue comes at the end of a story, after the main action.`,`True`,[`False`],`An epilogue often shows what happened to the characters later; a prologue comes at the start.`]],[[3,2,`grammar`,`Which word is the opposite of up?`,`down`,[`over`,`high`,`top`],`Up and down are opposites: a lift goes up and then comes down.`],[4,1,`grammar`,`Which word is a verb: jump, chair, green or soft?`,`jump`,[`chair`,`green`,`soft`],`A verb is an action word; you can jump. Chair is a noun, and green and soft are adjectives.`],[5,1,`grammar`,`What do we call the words the, a and an?`,`Articles`,[`Pronouns`,`Adverbs`,`Prepositions`],`‘The’ is the definite article; ‘a’ and ‘an’ are indefinite articles.`],[5,2,`grammar`,`Ten ___ were grazing in the field. Which plural of sheep fills the gap?`,`sheep`,[`sheeps`,`sheepes`,`shoop`],`Sheep is the same in the singular and the plural, like deer and fish.`],[6,1,`grammar`,`What is an interjection?`,`A word that shows a sudden feeling, such as ‘Wow!’`,[`A word that joins two clauses`,`A word that names a place`,`A word that describes a verb`],`Interjections such as ‘Oh!’, ‘Ouch!’ and ‘Hooray!’ often stand alone with an exclamation mark.`],[7,1,`grammar`,`What is a compound word?`,`Two words joined to make one, such as football`,[`A word with a prefix`,`A very long word`,`A word borrowed from French`],`Sunflower, toothbrush and rainbow are compound words.`],[7,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence uses lie and lay correctly?`,`I lay the book on the table, then lie down.`,[`I lie the book on the table, then lay down.`,`I lay the book on the table, then lay down.`,`I lie the book on the table, then lie down.`],`You lay something down (it takes an object); you lie down yourself.`],[8,2,`grammar`,`Which sentence uses among correctly?`,`She shared the sweets among her five friends.`,[`She sat among the two doors.`,`The secret stayed among you and me.`,`Choose among red or blue.`],`‘Among’ is for three or more; ‘between’ is usually for two.`],[3,1,`vocabulary`,`What do you call a place where you can see animals from around the world?`,`A zoo`,[`A farm`,`A library`,`A museum`],`Zoos keep and care for wild animals so people can learn about them.`],[3,3,`vocabulary`,`Which word means very, very cold?`,`freezing`,[`warm`,`mild`,`boiling`],`Freezing is cold enough for water to turn to ice.`],[4,1,`vocabulary`,`What is a group of people who sing together called?`,`A choir`,[`A team`,`A crew`,`A herd`],`A choir sings together, often in harmony.`],[4,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to share out among several people?`,`distribute`,[`collect`,`hoard`,`borrow`],`The teacher distributed the books to the class.`],[5,1,`vocabulary`,`Which word means a feeling of very great fear?`,`terror`,[`joy`,`boredom`,`pride`],`Terror is extreme fear, stronger than worry or nervousness.`],[5,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word means a person who lives in a particular place?`,`resident`,[`visitor`,`tourist`,`stranger`],`Residents live there; visitors and tourists only stay for a while.`],[6,1,`vocabulary`,`If food is abundant, there is…`,`plenty of it, more than enough`,[`none of it left`,`only a little`,`too little to share`],`Abundant means present in large amounts.`],[6,2,`vocabulary`,`A diligent student is one who…`,`works hard and carefully`,[`is often late`,`is very forgetful`,`talks during lessons`],`Diligence means steady, careful effort.`],[7,1,`vocabulary`,`A referee who is impartial…`,`treats both sides fairly`,[`supports the home team`,`is new to the job`,`never blows the whistle`],`Impartial means not favouring either side.`],[7,2,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to introduce new ideas or methods?`,`innovate`,[`imitate`,`repeat`,`abandon`],`To innovate is to make changes by bringing in something new.`],[8,1,`vocabulary`,`Which word describes writing that uses few words to say a lot?`,`concise`,[`verbose`,`vague`,`repetitive`],`Concise writing is brief and clear; verbose writing uses too many words.`],[8,2,`vocabulary`,`An audacious plan is…`,`bold and daring`,[`dull and ordinary`,`careful and slow`,`secret and hidden`],`Audacious means willing to take surprising risks.`],[8,3,`vocabulary`,`Which word means to make a problem or a bad situation worse?`,`exacerbate`,[`alleviate`,`resolve`,`postpone`],`Scratching an insect bite can exacerbate the itching; cool water may alleviate it.`],[3,2,`spelling`,`How do you spell the number that comes after seven?`,`eight`,[`ate`,`eigth`,`aight`],`Eight has a silent gh: e-i-g-h-t. Ate is the past tense of eat.`],[5,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for the people watching a show is correct?`,`audience`,[`audiance`,`awdience`,`audeince`],`Audience comes from Latin audire, to hear.`],[6,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning to get or gain something is correct?`,`acquire`,[`aquire`,`acquier`,`accquire`],`Acquire has a c before the q: ac-quire.`],[7,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word for a special right or advantage is correct?`,`privilege`,[`priviledge`,`privelege`,`privilige`],`Privilege has no d, and the vowels go i, i, e, e.`],[8,1,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning easy to see or notice is correct?`,`noticeable`,[`noticable`,`noteceable`,`noticeible`],`The e of notice stays before -able, so that the c keeps its soft ‘s’ sound.`],[8,2,`spelling`,`Which spelling of the word meaning to own or have is correct?`,`possess`,[`posess`,`possesss`,`pocess`],`Possess has two double s's: po-ss-e-ss.`],[6,2,`punctuation`,`In a list whose items already contain commas, which mark separates the items?`,`Semicolons`,[`Full stops`,`Apostrophes`,`Question marks`],`‘We visited Sylhet, Bangladesh; Kolkata, India; and Kathmandu, Nepal.’`],[7,3,`punctuation`,`Which sentence uses an exclamation mark sensibly?`,`Watch out for that car!`,[`What time is it!`,`I think it might rain later!`,`The meeting is on Tuesday!`],`Exclamation marks suit strong feeling or warnings, not calm statements or questions.`],[4,1,`figurative`,`If someone groans ‘My feet are killing me!’, what do they mean?`,`Their feet hurt a lot`,[`Their feet are dangerous`,`Their shoes are too big`,`They want to go running`],`It is an exaggeration (hyperbole) for being very sore.`],[5,1,`figurative`,`People say ‘once bitten, twice shy’. What does the saying warn about?`,`After a bad experience, you are more careful`,[`Dogs bite twice`,`Shy people are often bitten`,`Try everything twice`],`Being hurt once makes people cautious the next time.`],[6,1,`figurative`,`Advice to ‘let sleeping dogs lie’ tells you to…`,`leave a settled problem alone to avoid trouble`,[`never wake up too early`,`keep your pets indoors`,`tell the truth at all times`],`Stirring up an old problem might cause new trouble.`],[7,1,`figurative`,`Someone who has ‘a chip on their shoulder’…`,`holds a grudge and feels unfairly treated`,[`has hurt their shoulder`,`is very hungry`,`is good at carrying things`],`The phrase describes lasting resentment, often about an old slight.`],[8,1,`figurative`,`Which idea lies behind the saying ‘the pen is mightier than the sword’?`,`Writing and ideas can be more powerful than force`,[`Pens are made of stronger metal`,`Soldiers should carry pens`,`Writing is more dangerous than fighting`],`Words can change minds and history in ways weapons cannot.`],[5,1,`reading`,`What is a prologue?`,`An introduction that comes before the main story`,[`The last chapter of a book`,`The list of characters`,`A review of the book`],`A prologue sets the scene; an epilogue comes at the end.`],[6,1,`reading`,`What is an ode?`,`A poem praising a person, thing or idea`,[`A very short joke`,`A newspaper headline`,`A letter to a friend`],`John Keats wrote ‘Ode to a Nightingale’.`],[7,1,`reading`,`What is satire?`,`Humour used to mock and criticise people or ideas`,[`A kind of gentle love poem`,`A true story of someone's life`,`A book of maps and charts`],`Jonathan Swift's ‘Gulliver's Travels’ satirises the politics of his time.`],[8,1,`reading`,`What is an anthology?`,`A collection of poems or stories by different writers`,[`A single very long novel`,`A dictionary of people's names`,`An atlas of maps and charts`],`School poetry books are often anthologies.`],[8,2,`reading`,`What is stream-of-consciousness writing?`,`Writing that follows a character's flowing thoughts`,[`Writing about rivers and streams`,`A list of facts in order`,`A script for a radio play`],`Virginia Woolf and James Joyce used it to show the mind at work.`]]],i=[...e(`english`,n),...r.flatMap(e=>t(`english`,e))];export{i as QUESTIONS};